macos-flutter failed 5 of the last 11 native-backend-gates runs, including twice
in a row on 2022766. Not a regression from recent work: it also failed on
8536a3a and 7070c1e, spanning commits before and after the runner consolidation.

MECHANISM, from the retained job log (run on 2022766, job 91354282915):

  09:52:39  Retrying the built Flutter application after the bounded VM-service stall
  09:52:59  Xcode build done.   7.0s
  09:57:59  output contract idle timeout after 300 seconds

The build takes SEVEN SECONDS on the retry and then flutter drive emits nothing
at all for the full 300. The app does launch: the simulator log shows the Runner
process, preceded by

  Runner[12880:9d6a] (libCoreFSCache.dylib) Errors found! Invalidating cache...

run-flutter-drive.sh already names this failure in its own header: "Flutter's
iOS launcher waits indefinitely when a launched app never publishes its
VM-service URI." So the mode was known and a retry existed.

WHY THE RETRY DID NOT HELP. It retried with --no-build on the SAME simulator.
with-ios-simulator.sh creates exactly one device and wraps the whole script, so
the UDID is fixed across both attempts. The retry therefore re-tested the build,
which was never at fault, while holding the wedged device constant. Both
attempts stalled identically, which is exactly what that predicts.

FIX: erase and reboot the simulator before retrying. Erase keeps the same UDID,
so the outer cleanup and its deletion audit still apply unchanged. The boot wait
is bounded at 60 polls of 2 seconds and says so if it gives up, because an
unbooted device would fail the retry for a different reason and hide the one
being fixed.

VERIFIED on a real device before shipping, not reasoned about:

  created 4584C408-E838-4171-BF55-ED33FE91FC7B
  state before: Booted
  erase exit=0
  rebooted: yes
  probe simulator deleted

HONEST BOUND. This is a well-founded hypothesis backed by the mechanism and the
log, not a proven cure: the stall is intermittent, so the only proof is the
failure rate over the next several runs. If it persists, the next variable to
take is the runtime itself rather than the device.

A note on my own measurement while diagnosing this: I first reported "retry
attempts logged: 0" after grepping a log fetch that had returned a two-line
BlobNotFound stub. The grep was counting nothing. The retry does fire. Same
vacuous-measurement class this repository keeps paying for, met inside the
diagnosis of another one.
